  "Metadata": {
    "RepoURL": "/Users/halawany/work/personal/mohammed/new_project",
    "Commit": "f8983f6f379d0db58e043a5afa1269cd1cd8175e",
    "CommitMsg": "Gate-2.2 C16-R3.4.4: a sample cannot establish a property of a set\n\nR3.4.3 read the receipts, but read them shallowly, and the shallowness was\nload-bearing: it validated the FIRST FIVE packages of each result and accepted\nany nonempty string as a PURL. Truncating a genuine 229-package filesystem\nresult to five passed. So did truncating it to one. So did corrupting every\npackage from index five onward. The constant was called PACKAGE_SAMPLE and its\ncomment argued a fabricated array \"fails on its first entries\" — which assumes\na forger who corrupts from the beginning.\n\nA. PACKAGE_SAMPLE is deleted. Every package in every result is validated, and\n   every PURL is parsed with the same exact-pinned packageurl-js@2.0.1 the C16\n   closure uses: canonical round-trip, name and version agreeing with the\n   record, ecosystem matching the analyzer (pnpm→npm, alpine→apk,\n   gobinary→golang), no duplicate identities. The composition rule is\n   ecosystem-specific because the data is: for apk the PURL namespace is the\n   DISTRO, so `pkg:apk/alpine/busybox` is the package `busybox`, not\n   `alpine/busybox`. Coverage is measured against SOURCE in both directions —\n   every reported package must exist in the lockfile universe, and every\n   production registry package the C16 closure derives must appear in the\n   report. Both bounds are derived and reported as measured, never written down.\n\nB. Result identities are unique. An image must declare its OS family and\n   release, its os-pkgs Type must equal that family, and its target must equal\n   `\u003cderivedRef\u003e (\u003cFamily\u003e \u003cName\u003e)` exactly — startsWith let `\u003cexactRef\u003e-attacker`\n   through. Every finding is validated: canonical severity, parsed package\n   identity, and correspondence with a package the SAME result reported. The\n   filesystem scan runs at HIGH,CRITICAL and passed, so any finding at all\n   contradicts its own verdict. Image scans now DECLARE `--scanners vuln,secret`\n   in the producer and the source-owned argv contract, and both must appear in\n   the stderr receipt. A secret fails closed: governing one would mean recording\n   that a credential in a shipped image is acceptable, which needs its own typed\n   mechanism, not a reuse of the vulnerability dispositions.\n\nC. The table is compared as a multiset with exact header order and real counts.\n   R3.4.3 built a Map keyed by target, so a forged FIRST row reporting 999/99/88\n   was silently overwritten by the genuine zero row that followed it and was\n   never examined. Rows that do not parse are now fatal rather than filtered.\n\nD. The audit must agree with itself: every severity counter zero, the advisory\n   object empty, dependency metadata typed and non-vacuous, and its total equal\n   to the source-derived lockfile universe. `low: 1` beside an empty advisory\n   set passed before, as did `info: 10`, as did an audit that examined nothing.\n\nE. receipt-invariants.test.ts: 24 controls on REAL data — the image results are\n   the captured scanner output from the delivered evidence, the filesystem set\n   is the whole derived lockfile universe. 22 close a reproduced false pass\n   against a frozen byte copy of R3.4.3; 1 is the positive baseline; 1 (a\n   reordered table header) is recorded, not claimed, because R3.4.3 rejected it\n   too.\n\nF. EXCEPTIONS.md G5 records the PACKAGE_SAMPLE claim and corrects the R3.4.3 §F\n   arithmetic: 18 tests are 1 baseline + 16 closures + 1 already-caught, not\n   \"17 closures plus one recorded case\". Also recorded: four frozen fixtures\n   shipped frozen COPIES of the shared argv contract, which makes a producer\n   change look like a verifier disagreement. Three now read the live contract;\n   the R3.4 copy is retained because it freezes the pre-R3.4.1 path convention\n   its controls exercise, and says so.\n\nVerified: 575/575 gate, 589/589 api unit, 297/297 integration and 58/58\nacceptance on a recreated database, 203/203 contracts, 3/3 tokens, 10/10\nPlaywright on a virgin database, typecheck, build, boundaries, lint.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e",
    "Author": "Halawany \u003ceahmad96@icloud.com\u003e",
    "Committer": "Halawany \u003ceahmad96@icloud.com\u003e"
  },
